November 28, 2010.

This is "ggg", a gglcars-based stroboscopic tuner, but redone for
Android.  Given a catchy name "TunerTime" (yay!  it's tuner time!)
because it is eventually destined for release on the Android Market,
where perhaps one of my programs will be used by more than one person.

Hah.

Anyways, I have figured out what I need to do.

android.media.AudioRecord gives me access to the microphone via read().
It is, for example, the interface used by sipdroid (open source sip
phone), so it must be pretty alright at least.  I will call its blocking
read() function over and over in a separate thread I will spawn for just
this purpose.  And that separate thread will have a Surface (or
SurfaceHolder?  I'm not sure) that it will hold onto, and that thread
will somehow copy pixels from the audio data to the Surface (through a
Parcel??).  The rest is simple UI garbage.

A real question, though, is whether it is worth keeping any JNI in this
at all.  The math involved is so simple that it's basically just a big
copy operation.  Though I think honestly that the entire thread could
live from start to finish in C, just always hanging in the JNI method
call to AudioRecord.read(), or into the Surface call to receive a parcel.

I guess I have to design a UI now.  The original has a 3-octave
stroboscopic view surrounded by buttons:
   * QUIT (unneeded on android - user will click 'home' or what-have-you)
   * PAUSE (can be just a tap of the stroboscopic display to pause/unpause, but needs PAUSE notifier!)
   * CONF -> changes other buttons to be +/- for center A (440Hz +/-1Hz
     increments), and +/- for stretch factor (5-digit decimal incremented
     by 25), and RESET (A=440, stretch=0)
   * +/- for octave, semi, and cent.
Plus a status display that provides:
   * A 3     <--- note octave
   * +0c     <--- cents
   * A=440   <--- center A frequency
   * +00005  <--- stretch factor

So I need to do a portrait mode display...

[strobe octave 1] ...
.....................
[strobe octave 2] ...
.....................
[strobe octave 3] ...
.....................
semi+ oct+  cent+ A=440
 A     3     +0c  str=00025
semi- oct-  cent- [CONF]


...

Okay, throwing together the base UI was pretty easy.  It is now fully
functional, except that it doesn't have the thread that does all the
stroboscopic stuff.  So it doesn't do anything, in other words.

